result:
Fixed. The bug was in how tokio built the abstract socket address:

**`tokio/src/net/unix/listener.rs:82`** (`UnixListener::bind`) and **`tokio/src/net/unix/stream.rs:78`** (`UnixStream::connect`) detected a leading NUL to identify an abstract path, then handed the *entire* byte string — NUL included — to `SocketAddr::from_abstract_name`. That constructor prepends the NUL itself, so `"\0aaa"` was registered as the abstract name `"\0aaa"`, i.e. `sun_path` = `\0\0aaa`. The socket worked (both bind and connect were wrong in the same way, so they matched each other), but the registered name didn't match what the caller asked for, and `local_addr().as_abstract_name()` came back as `b"\0aaa"` instead of `b"aaa"`.

Both sites now strip the prefix before passing the name along:

```rust
let os_str_bytes = path.as_ref().as_os_str().as_bytes();
if let Some(name) = os_str_bytes.strip_prefix(b"\0") {
    // `from_abstract_name` prepends the leading null byte itself,
    // so it must be given the name without it.
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(name)?
} else {
    StdSocketAddr::from_pathname(path)?
}
```

Verification: `abstract_socket_name` fails on the unmodified tree (asserts `b"aaa"`, gets the NUL-prefixed name) and passes with the fix; the rest of `uds_stream` plus `uds_datagram`, `uds_socket`, `uds_split`, and `uds_cred` all pass. `UnixDatagram::bind` goes through mio's path-based API rather than this code path, so it was never affected.
